        </div>

        <p>
          Long rural driveways often price better per square foot than small suburban ones, because the fixed costs — mobilising the paver, the roller and the crew — spread across more area. A 200-square-foot job can carry a minimum charge that pushes its effective rate well above the range above.
        </p>

        <h2 id="new-overlay-repair" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. New Install, Overlay or Repair?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Three different jobs get called "a new driveway", and they differ by a factor of five in price. Getting this classification right is worth more than any negotiating.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Full Replacement — $4 to $10 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          Everything comes out, the subgrade is regraded and compacted, a fresh aggregate base goes down, and new asphalt is laid over it. This is the right call when the base has failed: potholes that return, alligator cracking across large areas, or sections that sink and hold water after rain.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Overlay — $2 to $4 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          A fresh mat of asphalt, typically one and a half to two inches, laid over the existing surface. It costs roughly half a replacement and takes a fraction of the time. The condition is that the base underneath must be sound: an overlay is a new wearing surface, not a structural repair, and laid over a failing base it will telegraph the same cracks within a couple of seasons. Height also matters — an overlay raises the surface, which can cause problems at the garage threshold and where the driveway meets the road.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Repair and Sealing — $0.25 to $2 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          Crack filling, pothole patching and sealcoating. On a driveway whose surface is intact but tired, this is by far the best money in the whole category, and it is the maintenance that determines whether you get fifteen years or thirty out of the asphalt. Our guides on <a href="/how-to-fix-crumbling-asphalt-driveway/">fixing a crumbling asphalt driveway</a> and <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/">choosing a crack filler</a> cover the work itself.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Removal — $1 to $3 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          Cheaper than concrete demolition because asphalt breaks up easily and is widely recycled. Many contractors mill and reuse it, and where the material can stay on site as compacted base, the price often improves.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-drives-price" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. What Drives the Price
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Compacted Thickness</h3>
        <p>
          Residential driveways want two to three inches of <strong>compacted</strong> asphalt, and that word is where quotes get slippery. Hot mix loses roughly a quarter of its depth as the roller passes, so three inches compacted starts as about four inches laid. A quote that promises "three inches of asphalt" without saying which figure it means is worth a phone call.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Base</h3>
        <p>
          Six to eight inches of compacted crushed aggregate is standard for residential work, more on soft or clay soils. As with concrete, the base is invisible in the finished job and it is where cheap quotes economise. Nearly every asphalt failure that looks like a surface problem — alligator cracking, potholes that keep coming back, depressions that puddle — is a base problem showing through.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Drainage and Grade</h3>
        <p>
          Water is what destroys asphalt. Standing water softens the binder and, in freezing climates, expands in every crack it finds. A driveway that sheds water properly can outlast a thicker one that ponds, so grading work in a quote is a feature rather than padding.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Access and Site Constraints</h3>
        <p>
          A driveway a paver can drive straight onto is the cheap case. Tight gates, steep slopes, and awkward corners mean more hand work, which is slower and produces a less uniform mat. Very steep drives may also need a stiffer mix so the surface does not creep in summer heat.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mix Type</h3>
        <p>
          Most residential driveways use a standard dense-graded hot mix, and the surface course uses a finer aggregate than the binder course underneath it for a smoother finish. Some contractors offer a modified or polymer-enhanced mix that resists rutting and cracking better, typically for a modest premium per ton. It is worth asking about on a steep drive, in a very hot climate, or where heavy vehicles park in the same spot every day, and it is largely wasted money on a flat suburban driveway in a temperate region.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Season and Plant Availability</h3>
        <p>
          Hot mix has to be laid hot and compacted before it cools, so asphalt has a working season. In northern regions plants close for winter entirely. Prices ease at the shoulders of the season rather than in peak summer, and a job scheduled for early autumn often prices better than the same job in June.
        </p>

        <h2 id="lifetime" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Twenty-Year Bill Nobody Quotes
        </h2>

        <p>
          Asphalt's real cost is the install plus the sealing cycle, and leaving the second half out is how the comparison against concrete gets distorted.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sealcoating is needed every two to four years, and never in the first year — new asphalt needs six to twelve months to cure before the first coat, and sealing too early traps volatiles and softens the surface. Professional sealing typically runs <strong>$0.25 to $0.75 per square foot</strong>; doing it yourself is closer to <strong>$0.15 to $0.30 per square foot</strong> in materials. Our <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">driveway sealing cost guide</a> breaks that down further.
        </p>

        <p>
          On a 600-square-foot driveway sealed professionally every three years at $0.45 per square foot, that is roughly $270 a time, or about $1,800 across twenty years — comfortably a third of what the driveway cost to install. Doing it yourself cuts that to something like $600 in materials over the same period, which is the strongest financial argument for owning a squeegee.
        </p>

        <p>
          Add crack filling as needed, the occasional pothole patch, and eventual replacement at 15 to 20 years, and the honest twenty-year figure for a 600-square-foot asphalt driveway lands somewhere near $4,500 to $8,500 all in. That is still usually less than concrete, but not by the margin the install quotes suggest.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">A Worked Example</h3>
        <p>
          Take the standard 600-square-foot two-car driveway. A mid-range install at $7 per square foot is $4,200. The first seal coat comes at around month twelve; after that, sealing every three years at $0.45 per square foot professionally is $270 a time, so six seal coats across twenty years is roughly $1,620. Allow another $300 across that period for crack filler and the occasional patch, and the twenty-year total lands near $6,100 — with a replacement due at the end of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same driveway in concrete at $13 per square foot is $7,800 up front, with sealing optional and far less frequent. Call it $8,500 across the same twenty years, on a slab with another decade or more of life left in it. That is the real shape of the comparison: asphalt costs less to own for the first ten years and the gap narrows steadily after that, until at the point asphalt needs replacing the concrete is only half way through its life.
        </p>

        <h2 id="vs-concrete" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Against Concrete
        </h2>

        <p>
          Concrete typically runs $8 to $18 per square foot installed against asphalt's $4 to $10, so the install gap is real and large. What closes it is time: concrete commonly lasts 30 years or more with occasional sealing, while asphalt needs its recurring seal coats and reaches the end of its life sooner.
        </p>

        <p>
          Climate is the tiebreaker more often than budget. In severe freeze-thaw regions asphalt's flexibility handles frost movement that cracks a rigid slab, and it tolerates de-icing salt that spalls concrete surfaces. In hot southern climates the reverse applies: asphalt softens in extreme heat and can rut under a parked vehicle, where concrete is unaffected. Our <a href="/asphalt-vs-concrete-driveway/">asphalt vs concrete comparison</a> takes the whole decision apart, and the <a href="/concrete-driveway-cost/">concrete driveway cost guide</a> gives the matching numbers.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is also a resale dimension that rarely gets mentioned. In most northern markets buyers expect asphalt and think nothing of it, so a tidy, recently sealed asphalt driveway is simply a neutral feature. In warmer markets where concrete dominates, an asphalt driveway can read as the cheaper option to a buyer comparing two similar houses. Neither effect is large enough to drive the decision, but if you are choosing partly with an eye on selling in a few years, it is worth knowing which surface your street already expects.
        </p>

        <h2 id="quotes" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Reading a Quote Properly
        </h2>

        <p>
          Five specifics turn a lump sum into something comparable.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Compacted thickness, stated as compacted.</strong> Two to three inches for residential. If the quote says "laid" thickness, mentally remove a quarter.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Base depth and material.</strong> Six to eight inches of compacted crushed aggregate is the residential standard. A bid with no base line is not comparable to one that has it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Whether the base is being reused or replaced.</strong> On a replacement, reusing a sound existing base legitimately saves money; on a failed base, reusing it guarantees the new surface fails the same way.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Edges and transitions.</strong> How the driveway meets the road, the garage threshold and the lawn. Unsupported asphalt edges crumble first, and a good contractor will say how they are handling them.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>What is excluded.</strong> Demolition, permits, drainage and grading are commonly separate lines. Most of the difference between two bids lives here rather than in the asphalt.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pro Tip: Be Suspicious of the Truck With Leftover Asphalt</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
            The classic driveway scam is a crew knocking on the door offering a cheap job with "material left over from a nearby project". Real hot mix is ordered for a specific job and cools on a clock; a load that has been driving around looking for a buyer is past its usable temperature and will never compact properly. Anyone selling a driveway door to door is selling you a surface that will fail in a season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying an Asphalt Driveway</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Overlay Over a Failed Base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An overlay is a wearing surface, not a structural repair. Alligator cracking and returning potholes mean the base has gone, and new asphalt over it reproduces the same failure within a season or two at half the price of doing it properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Too Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New asphalt needs six to twelve months before the first seal coat. Sealing a fresh driveway traps volatiles and leaves a soft surface that scuffs and tracks. Nothing is gained by sealing early, and the surface pays for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Too Often</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Annual sealcoating builds excess thickness that cracks in sheets. Two to four years is the interval; more frequent coats cost money and actively shorten the surface's life.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking Heavy Vehicles on a Thin Mat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two inches of compacted asphalt is a car driveway. An RV, a loaded trailer or a work truck will depress it, especially in summer heat. If heavy vehicles are in the picture, buy the extra inch at the quoting stage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Cracks Through a Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every crack is a route for water into the base, and water that freezes there lifts the surface. A tube of crack filler in autumn is the highest-return maintenance available on an asphalt driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does an asphalt driveway cost?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Most markets run $4 to $10 per square foot installed, with straightforward jobs in low-cost regions closer to $3. A typical 600-square-foot two-car driveway therefore lands between about $2,400 and $6,000, before demolition of any existing surface.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is asphalt cheaper than concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">On installation, yes — typically 40 to 60 percent less. The gap narrows over time: asphalt needs resealing every two to four years and generally reaches the end of its life at 15 to 20 years, where concrete commonly runs 30 or more with much less maintenance.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should an asphalt driveway be?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Two to three inches of compacted asphalt over six to eight inches of compacted aggregate base for residential use. Note the word compacted — hot mix loses about a quarter of its depth under the roller, so three inches compacted starts as roughly four laid. Heavy vehicles justify four inches compacted.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does an asphalt overlay cost?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Typically $2 to $4 per square foot — around half the price of full replacement. It only works where the existing base is sound; over a failing base the new mat telegraphs the same cracks within a couple of seasons. It also raises the surface, which matters at the garage threshold and the road.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does removal cost?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Usually $1 to $3 per square foot — cheaper than concrete demolition, because asphalt breaks up easily and is widely recycled. Many contractors mill and reuse it, and the price often improves if the material can stay on site as base.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does asphalt need sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Every two to four years, and never in the first year — new asphalt needs six to twelve months to cure first. Professional sealing runs about $0.25 to $0.75 per square foot; doing it yourself is closer to $0.15 to $0.30 in materials.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an asphalt driveway last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Fifteen to twenty years with regular sealing and prompt crack repair, and up to thirty on a well-built base in a mild climate. Inadequate base, standing water, heavy vehicles on a thin mat, and cracks left open through a winter are what shorten it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What time of year is cheapest?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">The shoulders of the working season rather than peak summer. Asphalt plants close in cold weather in northern regions, so winter is often not available at any price — and hot mix laid in cold conditions will not compact properly regardless of the discount.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

