        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Budget <strong class="text-white">$4 to $10 per square foot</strong> for a new asphalt driveway, <strong class="text-white">$2 to $4</strong> for an overlay where the base is sound, and <strong class="text-white">$1 to $3</strong> to remove the old surface. For a standard two-car driveway that is roughly $2,400 to $6,000 installed — genuinely cheaper than concrete.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Then plan for the part the quote leaves out: a seal coat every two to four years, crack filling each autumn, and replacement somewhere around year twenty. Do that maintenance and asphalt is excellent value. Skip it and you will be paying the install price again far sooner than the surface deserved.
          </p>
        </div>

